.TH ONEDRIVE-CPP 1 "7 October 2026" "onedrive-cpp @ONEDRIVE_PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
onedrive-cpp \- modern C++ OneDrive synchronization client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B onedrive-cpp
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp auth
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp logout
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp doctor
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp drives
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp shared
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp sites
.RI [ options ]
.I QUERY
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp quota
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp status
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp state reset-cursor
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp state clear
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp download
.RI [ options ]
.I REMOTE_PATH
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp sync
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B onedrive-cpp monitor
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B onedrive-cpp
is an independent, open-source client intended to authenticate with Microsoft
and synchronize files through Microsoft Graph and OneDrive.
.PP
The current release provides bidirectional Microsoft Graph synchronization:
Delta queries and explicit downloads apply remote creations, changes, moves,
and deletions locally, while local scanning uploads new and modified files,
creates directories, and conditionally propagates deletions, moves, and
renames. SQLite journals provide crash recovery for transfer and metadata
operations. The client also includes device-code authentication, selective
synchronization, dry-run and JSON output, inotify monitoring, proxy support,
rate limiting, shell completion, and packaging integration.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B auth
Authorize the application with Microsoft using the OAuth 2.0 device-code flow.
On success, save the refresh token in the configured state directory.
.TP
.B logout
Remove the locally stored refresh token. This does not revoke consent on the
Microsoft account; revoke the application separately in Microsoft account
settings when required.
.TP
.B doctor
Run a read-only SQLite
.BR integrity_check ,
foreign-key check, and exact application-schema check for every Drive database
under the configured state directory. The command does not contact Microsoft
Graph and returns nonzero when any database is unhealthy. Use
.B \-\-output json
for structured diagnostics.
.TP
.B drives
List OneDrive drives available to the active Microsoft account and mark the
configured drive. The command reads Microsoft Graph metadata without changing
remote or local synchronization state.
.TP
.B shared
List items shared with the active account and shortcuts added to the configured
OneDrive. Output includes target Drive and item IDs, ownership, item kind, web
URL, and the local OneDrive path for shortcuts. The command performs read-only
Graph queries and does not synchronize content.
.TP
.B sites
Search accessible SharePoint sites matching
.I QUERY
and list their document-library Drives. This read-only command requires
.B Sites.Read.All
or
.B Sites.ReadWrite.All
in
.BR auth.scopes ;
run
.B onedrive-cpp auth
again after changing scopes.
.TP
.B quota
Show total, used, remaining, deleted, and quota-state values for the configured
drive. The command does not run synchronization.
.TP
.B status
Show the active account and canonical Drive identity, synchronization mode and
delete policy, last recorded synchronization result, tracked and blocked item
counts, pending journals, Delta cursor, selective-sync fingerprint, state
database availability, and WebSocket configuration. Existing SQLite state is
opened read-only and is never created or migrated by this command.
.TP
.B state reset-cursor
Reset the saved delta link for the configured drive. This preserves
authentication, configuration, local files, item snapshots, pending-download,
partial-download, pending-upload, pending-move recovery records, selectively
retained upload suppressions, blocked-item records, and state for other drives. The next
.B sync
recovers pending downloads and performs a full initial delta query for the
configured drive. The complete remote inventory replaces old item metadata;
items that still conflict are recorded as blocked.
.TP
.B state clear
remove item snapshots, the delta link, pending-download, partial-download,
pending-upload, and pending-move recovery records, selectively retained upload
suppressions, and blocked items for the configured drive. The command requires the configured
drive ID as interactive confirmation. Local files and other drives remain
untouched, but the next sync may report local modification conflicts. For automation,
.B \-\-yes
skips the interactive confirmation.
.PP
At startup and after migration, each Drive database must pass SQLite
.B quick_check
and match the application table, column, NOT NULL, and primary-key-index
schema generated by this client. Synchronization stops before item state is
used when the database is unversioned but nonempty, newer than the client, or
structurally incompatible. Confirmed corruption is quarantined as
.I items.sqlite3.corrupt\-<timestamp>
with its WAL/SHM sidecars before a reconstructible database is created.
Local files remain untouched, but the next sync may report local modification
conflicts without the old snapshots.
.TP
.B download
Download one file identified by a Drive-relative
.IR REMOTE_PATH .
Unsafe path components are rejected before a Graph request. The command reuses
the normal eTag precondition, HTTPS redirects, Range transfers, durable
checkpoints, integrity validation, disk-space reservation, local-modification
protection, and atomic installation. Directories and files marked with the
Microsoft Graph malware facet are rejected. The item snapshot is updated
without advancing the Drive delta link. With
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
display the resolved remote path, local destination, and expected size without
creating the item database or changing downloaded files.
.TP
.B sync
Refresh the OAuth access token and retrieve recursive drive changes using the
Microsoft Graph delta API. Successful non-dry-run queries atomically persist
remote metadata and the final delta link after applying remote changes and
then scanning for local changes to upload. New and modified local files,
directories, deletions, moves, and renames use conditional Graph operations
and durable recovery journals. Downloads use same-directory temporary
files, size validation, flushing, and atomic replacement. Existing local files
that cannot be proven unchanged are persisted as blocked items instead of being
overwritten. Invalid names, local path conflicts, files marked with the
Microsoft Graph malware facet, and descendants of blocked directories are also
isolated while independent files continue. Malware-marked files are not
downloaded or allowed to replace local data. A partial completion exits with
status 2, and blocked items are retried on later syncs. Installed files use the
authoritative fileSystemInfo.lastModifiedDateTime value; files without a valid
value are rejected before download. Permissions are derived from mode 0666 and
the process umask.
.PP
The default
.B \-\-ui auto
mode displays an FTXUI status dashboard only when both standard streams are
terminals, terminal controls are supported, and the window is at least 60 by
12. Unsupported terminals, redirection, pipes, JSON, and quiet mode
automatically retain normal console output.
SQLite pending-download records and SHA-256 content fingerprints provide
crash recovery independently of target-file-system extended attributes.
Downloads starting at byte zero calculate SHA-256 and QuickXorHash while data
is written and reuse the streamed SHA-256 recovery fingerprint. Resumed or
non-contiguous transfers fall back to hashing the completed file.
Range chunks are accepted only when HTTP status, Content-Range, total size, and
received byte count exactly match the request; rejected chunks are rolled back
to the preceding durable offset.
Microsoft Graph HTTP 408, 429, 502, 503, and 504 responses are retried using
.B Retry-After
or bounded exponential backoff, with a finite retry limit. A pre-authenticated
download URL that returns HTTP 401 or 403 is refreshed through Graph once.
Remote deletion records remove regular local files only while their size and
modification time still match the trusted synchronized snapshot. Directories
are processed child-first and removed only when empty. Modified files,
symbolic links, unexpected path types, and nonempty directories are retained
as retryable blocked items. Missing targets are accepted. Full Delta refreshes
reconcile the Graph inventory with prior snapshots, while items excluded only
by selective synchronization retain their local files. A schema-v16 upload
suppression binds an included-to-excluded retained file to its device/inode,
preventing the same object from being uploaded again at its old path. Missing
or identity-replaced objects release stale suppressions during upload scanning.
.PP
Delta items that retain their remote ID while changing path are renamed or
moved locally without replacing an existing destination. Directory moves
remap tracked descendants even when Graph reports only the directory.
Dependent moves vacate occupied destinations first, and parent directory moves
remap the effective source of explicit child renames. Name exchanges and other
dependency cycles move one breaker to a private hidden staging path, install
the remaining moves in dependency order, and then install the staged item.
Content-identical files are reused; files whose remote content also changed
are downloaded after the move. Locally modified sources, symbolic links,
unexpected types, and occupied destinations are persisted as retryable blocked
items. Durable parent-directory flushes and destination adoption make an
interrupted move safe to retry.
Moves across filesystem boundaries are persisted as
.B cross_device_move
blocked items; data is not copied and deleted across mount points.
Before rename, a schema-v16 SQLite pending-move journal records the source,
destination, optional staging path, and source device/inode identity. The moved
item, Delta cursor, and journal removal commit atomically. Recovery searches
the original, staging, and destination paths for the recorded identity.
.PP
Use
.B \-\-dry\-run
to display the download plan without creating files or updating synchronization
state.
.TP
.B monitor
Run an initial synchronization, then monitor the local Drive tree recursively
with inotify and poll Microsoft Graph periodically. Local event bursts are
coalesced before synchronization. SIGINT and SIGTERM stop the monitor cleanly
after the active synchronization finishes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help for the application or selected subcommand and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display the application version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
With
.BR "state clear" ,
confirm the destructive state clear without an interactive prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-force\-large\-delete
With
.BR sync ,
allow this invocation to exceed
.BR sync.maximum_remote_deletions .
The override is not available to
.B monitor
and cannot be persisted in the configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Read application configuration from
.IR PATH .
The default is
.I ~/.config/onedrive-cpp/config.toml
when
.B HOME
is available, otherwise
.IR /etc/onedrive-cpp/onedrive-cpp.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
Set the minimum log level. Supported values are
.BR trace ,
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.BR warn ,
.BR error ,
.BR critical ,
and
.BR off .
The default is
.BR info .
At
.B trace
level, HTTP transport diagnostics include the negotiated protocol, new
connection count, and DNS, TCP, TLS, server wait, body transfer, and total
timings. Request URLs, headers, and bodies are not included in these
diagnostics. HTTP requests disable libcurl signal use so concurrent transfers
report connection failures through normal errors instead of process signals.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-file " PATH"
Also write logs to
.IR PATH .
The file rotates at 5 MiB and retains three older files. Diagnostics continue
to be written to standard error.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " MODE"
Control ANSI styling. Supported modes are
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
and
.BR never .
The default
.B auto
enables styling only when standard output is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is not set.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " FORMAT"
Select
.B text
or
.B json
command output. JSON output uses one compact object per line, never contains
ANSI sequences, and requires
.B \-\-yes
for destructive operations that would otherwise prompt interactively.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress informational and success command output while retaining warnings
and errors. Diagnostic logging remains controlled separately by
.BR \-\-log\-level .
.TP
.BI \-\-ui " MODE"
Select the
.B sync
interface:
.BR auto ,
.BR console ,
or
.BR tui .
The default
.B auto
mode falls back to the normal console when a TUI is unavailable.
.B tui
instead reports an error when terminal requirements are not met.
For the monitor dashboard,
.BR q ,
.BR Q ,
and Escape request a clean shutdown.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " THEME"
Select the TUI color theme:
.BR hacker ,
.BR ocean ,
.BR amber ,
or
.BR synthwave .
The default hacker theme uses a bright green terminal palette. This option
overrides
.B console.theme
for one run. Color-disabled output retains the layout without theme colors.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
For
.BR download ,
resolve and display the single-file plan without creating the item database or
changing downloaded files. For
.B sync
, display the effective synchronization inputs without modifying remote files.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file uses TOML and must declare
.BR "config_version = 2" .
Unknown keys, invalid types, and unsupported configuration versions are
rejected.
Version 1 configuration files must move the former
.B sync.download_*
keys into the
.B transfer
and
.B download
tables.
.PP
Before a command runs, the state directory is restricted to mode 0700 and an
exclusive
.I onedrive-cpp.lock
is held there. Token and SQLite paths must be regular files owned by the
current user; private state files are restricted to mode 0600. Symbolic links
in state paths are rejected. SQLite databases and existing WAL/SHM files must
also be regular, single-link, current-user-owned files. Connections disable
trusted schema and extension loading, enable defensive and foreign-key modes,
apply parser and growth limits, wait briefly for locks, and use WAL with full
synchronous writes, automatic checkpoints, and fast secure deletion.
.PP
For synchronization, the sync and state directories must not overlap, the
filesystem root cannot be used as the sync directory, and symbolic-link path
components are rejected. Normal synchronization verifies create, write,
flush, and removal behavior with a temporary probe. Download capacity retains
a safety reserve of 256 MiB or five percent of planned transfer bytes,
whichever is larger.
.TP
.B sync.data_directory
Root directory for synchronized data. Actual content is stored under
.IR accounts/<display-name>--<user-id-hash>/drives/<drive-name>--<drive-id-hash>
so multiple Microsoft users and Drives remain isolated. Files from the former
flat layout are not moved automatically.
.TP
.B sync.permissions
Local permission policy for synchronized data.
.B private
creates files as 0600 and secures the synchronization root and new directories
as 0700.
.B umask
uses the process umask and is intended only for explicitly shared directories.
The default is
.BR private .
.TP
.B sync.local_conflict
Policy for simultaneous local and remote regular-file changes.
.B block
is the default and refuses the replacement.
.B backup
first makes a durable, independent same-directory copy named like
.I report.safeBackup-20261004T051000Z-0001.pdf
and then atomically installs the authoritative remote version. Content-identical
files are adopted without a backup or inode replacement. A backup retains local
permission bits, is not uploaded, and requires additional disk space equal to
the local file. Backup failure leaves the destination unchanged. This setting
applies to normal synchronization and the
.B download
command. If upload recovery discovers a simultaneous remote change, it
discards only the stale upload snapshot and journal, then reconciles the
remote delta through this same policy. Directory and remote-deletion conflicts
that cannot produce a safe regular-file copy remain blocked.
.TP
.B state.directory
Directory containing the refresh token and SQLite synchronization state.
.TP
.B sync.drive_id
Microsoft Graph drive identifier. The default is
.BR me .
.TP
.B sync.sync_list
Optional path to a UTF-8 selective-synchronization rule file. Relative paths
are resolved from the directory containing the TOML configuration file. When
configured, the file must be readable; an empty file selects no remote items.
The file excludes everything by default. Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored. Inclusion rules name selected paths, while
.B !
or
.B -
prefixes exclusion rules. Exclusions override inclusions. A leading
.B /
anchors a rule at the Drive root, a trailing
.B /
selects directories and their descendants,
.B *
matches within one path segment, and a complete
.B **
segment matches recursively. Invalid or unsafe path components are rejected.
Filtering occurs after Graph Delta metadata is fetched. A fingerprint of the
effective rules is stored atomically with the Delta cursor; changing or
removing the rules automatically triggers a full remote-state query. Existing
local files that become excluded are not deleted. The explicit
.B download
command is not filtered by this setting.
.TP
.B sync.nosync_enabled
When true (the default), a regular local file named
.B .nosync
excludes its containing directory and complete subtree from automatic
synchronization. The marker itself is never synchronized; a symbolic link is
not a marker. Adding or removing a marker updates the filter fingerprint and
forces a full Delta query.
.TP
.B sync.dotfiles
Set to
.B include
(the default) or
.BR exclude .
Exclusion applies when any path component begins with a dot and contains at
least one additional character.
.TP
.B sync.maximum_file_size_bytes
Maximum eligible regular-file size for both downloads and uploads. Zero (the
default) means unlimited. Files exactly equal to a positive limit remain
eligible. Filtered files do not cause remote deletions, and tracked local files
that become excluded are retained.
.TP
.B auth.application_id
Microsoft Entra Application (client) ID.
.TP
.B auth.tenant_id
Microsoft identity tenant selector, such as
.BR common ,
.BR consumers ,
or a specific tenant ID. Use
.B common
for an application registered for both organizational and personal accounts.
.TP
.B auth.endpoint
Microsoft identity endpoint. The default is
.IR https://login.microsoftonline.com .
.TP
.B auth.scopes
Array of delegated OAuth scope strings. Personal Microsoft accounts should
start with
.BR [\(dqUser.Read\(dq,\ \(dqFiles.ReadWrite\(dq,\ \(dqoffline_access\(dq] .
The broader
.B Files.ReadWrite.All
,
.B Sites.Read.All
and
.B Sites.ReadWrite.All
scopes must be configured explicitly and produce a warning during
authentication.
.TP
.B graph.endpoint
Microsoft Graph API endpoint. The default is
.IR https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0 .
Sovereign-cloud deployments must configure matching Graph and authentication
endpoints.
.TP
.B graph.throttle.maximum_retries
Maximum number of retries for a retryable Microsoft Graph API response. The
default is
.BR 4 .
.TP
.B graph.throttle.initial_delay_seconds
Initial exponential-backoff delay when a retryable response has no valid numeric
.B Retry-After
header. The default is
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B graph.throttle.maximum_delay_seconds
Maximum accepted or calculated Graph throttling delay. The default is
.B 300
seconds. This value must be greater than or equal to
.BR graph.throttle.initial_delay_seconds .
.TP
.B proxy.url
Optional proxy URL used for authentication, Microsoft Graph, and file-transfer
requests. Supported schemes are
.BR http ,
.BR https ,
.BR socks4 ,
.BR socks4a ,
.BR socks5 ,
and
.BR socks5h .
The default is unset, so the application does not explicitly select a proxy.
libcurl may still use
.BR HTTP_PROXY ,
.BR HTTPS_PROXY ,
.BR ALL_PROXY ,
and their lowercase variants from the environment. To guarantee direct
connections, omit the proxy table and unset those environment variables.
Use
.B socks5h
to resolve destination host names through the proxy. The
.B NO_PROXY
or
.B no_proxy
environment variable can bypass the proxy for matching destinations. Available
TLS and authentication mechanisms depend on the installed libcurl build.
.TP
.B proxy.no_proxy
Array of hosts, domain suffixes, IP addresses, or other libcurl no-proxy
patterns. When omitted, the
.B NO_PROXY
or
.B no_proxy
environment variable remains effective. An empty array overrides the
environment and sends every destination through the proxy.
.TP
.B proxy.username
Optional proxy authentication username. The default is unset, so no explicit
proxy credentials are configured.
.TP
.B proxy.password_file
File containing the proxy password. Relative paths resolve from the
configuration file directory. The file must be a regular file reached without
symbolic links, grant no group or other permissions, and be no larger than
64 KiB. One trailing LF or CRLF is removed. This setting requires
.BR proxy.username .
.TP
.B proxy.auth
HTTP or HTTPS proxy authentication mechanism:
.BR auto ,
.BR basic ,
.BR digest ,
.BR ntlm ,
or
.BR negotiate .
The default is
.BR auto .
SOCKS5 uses its own username/password authentication.
.TP
.B proxy.ca_file
Certificate-authority file for an HTTPS proxy. Relative paths resolve from the
configuration file directory. This setting is rejected for non-HTTPS proxy
URLs. The default is unset, so HTTPS proxies use the system CA trust store.
Proxy certificate and host verification remain enabled.
.TP
.B download.concurrency
Maximum number of files downloaded concurrently. The default is
.B 4
and the accepted range is 1 through 16. Downloads targeting the same
normalized local path remain serialized.
.TP
.B download.maximum_retries
Maximum number of retries for a file-content request after a transient HTTP or
transport failure. The default is
.BR 4 ;
zero disables file-content retries. Download retries use the Graph backoff
delay settings.
.TP
.B download.chunk_threshold_bytes
Files larger than this value use sequential HTTP Range requests with this
maximum chunk size. The default is
.B 8388608
bytes and the value must be greater than zero.
.TP
.B download.checkpoint_interval_bytes
Flush and record resumable download progress after this many newly downloaded
bytes. The default is
.B 1048576
bytes and the value must be greater than zero. Smaller values reduce repeated
downloads after interruption but increase synchronization and database
overhead.
.TP
.B transfer.order
Order in which file transfers enter the worker queue:
.BR default ,
.BR size_asc ,
.BR size_dsc ,
.BR name_asc ,
or
.BR name_dsc .
The default preserves synchronization-plan order, and equal sort keys remain
stable. With concurrent workers this controls start order rather than
completion order. The setting applies to the download queue; upload
dependencies retain their parent-first operation order.
.TP
.B transfer.connect_timeout_seconds
Connection timeout for each file-transfer request. The default is
.B 30
seconds and the value must be greater than zero.
.TP
.B transfer.operation_timeout_seconds
Maximum duration of each file-transfer request. The default is
.B 3600
seconds and the value must be greater than zero.
.TP
.B transfer.stall_timeout_seconds
Abort a transfer that remains below the configured minimum rate for this many
seconds. The default is
.BR 60 ;
zero disables stall detection.
.TP
.B transfer.stall_minimum_bytes_per_second
Minimum transfer rate used by stall detection. The default is
.B 1
byte per second and the value must be greater than zero.
.TP
.B download.maximum_rate_bytes_per_second
Maximum receive rate for each download. The default is
.BR 0 ,
which disables rate limiting.
.TP
.B download.maximum_total_rate_bytes_per_second
Combined receive rate shared by all concurrent file downloads. The default is
.BR 0 ,
which disables the total limit. When both download rate settings are non-zero,
each request is subject to the individual limit while all active requests share
the total limit. The total limiter permits a burst of at most 64 KiB.
Throttling time counts toward the operation timeout and may contribute to stall
detection, so very low limits may require longer timeout settings.
.TP
.B upload.chunk_size_bytes
Files through 250 MB (250,000,000 bytes) use a simple upload. Larger files use
an upload session. This option sets its fragment size; the default is 10 MiB.
The value must be a positive multiple of 320 KiB and less than 60 MiB.
.TP
.B upload.concurrency
Maximum number of independent files uploaded concurrently. The default is 1
and the accepted range is 1 through 16. Upload-session fragments for one file
remain sequential, and directories are created before dependent file uploads.
.TP
.B upload.maximum_rate_bytes_per_second
Maximum send rate for each upload request. The default is
.BR 0 ,
which disables the per-request limit.
.TP
.B upload.maximum_total_rate_bytes_per_second
Combined upload send-rate ceiling. The default is
.BR 0 .
All active uploads share a fair, cancellable token bucket while each request
also observes the per-upload limit.
.TP
.B monitor.poll_interval_seconds
Maximum quiet interval between Microsoft Graph delta checks. The default is
300 seconds. The value must be between 1 and 86400.
.TP
.B monitor.settle_delay_milliseconds
Time used to coalesce a burst of local inotify events before synchronizing.
The default is 1000 milliseconds and the maximum is 60000. Zero triggers
immediately after a completed actionable event.
.TP
.B transfer.http_version
HTTP version preference for file transfers:
.BR auto ,
.BR 1.1 ,
or
.BR 2 .
The default is
.BR auto .
.TP
.B transfer.ip_version
IP address family for file-transfer requests:
.BR auto ,
.BR 4 ,
or
.BR 6 .
The default is
.BR auto .
Forcing an address family can work around broken IPv6 or IPv4 routing, but the
request fails when the download host has no address in that family.
.TP
.B download.validation
Remote metadata validation policy:
.B strict
requires the downloaded size and any Graph-provided content hash to match;
.B relaxed
accepts SharePoint, AIP, or HEIC content that differs from Graph metadata and
disables resumable and chunked downloads. Relaxed downloads reserve disk space
incrementally from actual transfer progress and abort if that reservation
cannot grow safely. Both modes reject non-success HTTP response bodies before
writing them to the temporary file or a durable checkpoint. The default is
.BR strict .
Relaxed mode weakens integrity guarantees.
.PP
Before requesting file content, the client sends the expected remote eTag as
an
.B If-Match
precondition. HTTP 412 indicates that the remote version changed and is not
retried as a transient transfer failure. The next synchronization can then
plan the current remote version.
.PP
Preauthenticated download URLs may follow at most five additional redirects.
Every redirect target must use HTTPS, and Microsoft Graph authorization is
never attached to these CDN requests. Intermediate response headers and bodies
are isolated from the final response validation.
.TP
.B filesystem.metadata
File-system metadata policy:
.B auto
probes user extended attributes and otherwise uses only the SQLite recovery
journal;
.B xattr
requires extended-attribute support; and
.B database
never reads or writes extended attributes. The default is
.BR auto .
.TP
.B sync.sync_root_files
Boolean value controlling implicit selection of ordinary files located
directly in the Drive root when
.B sync.sync_list
is configured. The default is
.BR false .
Root directories and descendants still require inclusion rules, and explicit
exclusions override implicit root-file selection. The setting has no effect
without a sync list. Changing it updates the selective-sync fingerprint and
forces a full remote-state query.
.TP
.B sync.maximum_remote_deletions
Maximum number of tracked items that one local deletion plan may remove from
OneDrive. The default is
.BR 1000 .
A missing tracked directory counts itself and every tracked descendant even
when Microsoft Graph needs only one DELETE request for the parent. A value of
.B 0
blocks every remote deletion unless a one-shot
.B sync \-\-force\-large\-delete
override is used. Normal synchronization and monitor mode stop before a remote
DELETE when the limit is exceeded. Dry-run output reports the operation count,
affected tracked-item count, limit, and blocked status. Pending-delete recovery
uses the same protection.
.PP
For tracked files, the state database stores both Graph eTag and cTag values.
If the local snapshot is unchanged and a delta changes only the eTag while
retaining the same non-empty cTag, synchronization refreshes metadata without
downloading the file again. A missing or changed cTag conservatively falls
back to the eTag behavior. Folder decisions do not rely on cTag because some
SharePoint and OneDrive for Business responses omit it or report descendant
changes inconsistently.
.TP
.B sync.dry_run
Boolean value
.B true
or
.BR false .
.TP
.B sync.mode
Synchronization direction. The default is
.BR bidirectional ,
which downloads remote changes and uploads local changes.
.B upload_only
still records the Graph Delta baseline but does not download, move, or delete
local content.
.B download_only
applies remote changes without scanning local changes for upload or recovering
pending upload, remote-move, or remote-delete operations.
.TP
.B sync.delete_policy
Deletion propagation policy.
.B propagate
mirrors source deletions to the destination.
.B preserve
keeps destination items when their source disappears and is the safe default
for
.BR upload_only .
In
.BR download_only ,
.B propagate
safely removes unchanged tracked local items and blocks local conflicts for
retry.
.B preserve
keeps the local item but consumes the remote tombstone, removes its tracked
state, and clears an older blocked deletion.
.TP
.B sync.upload
Legacy compatibility boolean.
.B true
maps to
.BR "sync.mode = bidirectional" ;
.B false
maps to
.BR "sync.mode = download_only" .
It cannot be combined with
.BR sync.mode .
New files fail on remote name conflicts, and tracked modifications use the
saved eTag as an If-Match precondition. Uploads use stable private snapshots
and a durable SQLite recovery journal. Selected untracked local directories
are created remotely in parent-first order before their files, with
fail-on-conflict semantics and durable recovery. Files through 250 MB use
simple upload;
larger files use contiguous Graph upload-session fragments and validate
nextExpectedRanges before advancing. Session URLs are preauthorized and never
receive the Graph Authorization header. The SQLite pending-upload journal
persists the session URL, expiration, and confirmed offset. After a restart,
the client queries and resumes a valid session without resending confirmed
fragments. Expired sessions and HTTP 404/410 responses are replaced; a server
offset behind the durable checkpoint aborts the upload.
OneDrive quota responses and local upload read, permission, snapshot-space,
or I/O failures persist an actionable reason and attempt count in the same
journal. A failed item does not stop unrelated uploads. Recorded failures are
retried once per synchronization, cleared on success, and otherwise reported
as blocked.
Missing tracked local items are deleted remotely with the saved eTag as an
If-Match precondition. Parent-directory deletions cover tracked descendants.
A dedicated SQLite pending-delete journal makes completed Graph deletions
recoverable after interruption: HTTP 404 is an idempotent success, while
409/412 leaves tracked item state intact. Dry-run and paths outside the active
sync-list never issue remote deletions.
Tracked local files and directories persist their filesystem device and inode
identity. Downloads and uploads record it immediately, and normal non-dry-run
upload scans backfill older snapshots. This identity supports safe local-move
recognition without relying on size and timestamp matches. Recognized moves
use the saved eTag in a conditional Graph PATCH and a dedicated SQLite
journal. File modifications made with a move upload after the move commits;
directory moves atomically remap tracked descendants. Recovery adopts a
destination only when its remote ID and local filesystem identity match. The
client creates newly added destination parents from shallowest to deepest
before issuing the conditional move. Parent creation and move recovery retain
their separate durable journals.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Bash and Zsh completion definitions are installed with the package. They
complete subcommands, command options, enumerated option values, and paths for
file-valued options.
.PP
For an uninstalled development tree, enable Bash completion in the current
shell with:
.PP
.EX
source packaging/completions/onedrive-cpp.bash
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/onedrive-cpp/config.toml
Default per-user configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/onedrive-cpp/onedrive-cpp.toml
System-wide example and fallback configuration.
.TP
.I sync_list
Optional selective-synchronization rule file at the path configured by
.BR sync.sync_list .
.TP
.I *.safeBackup-YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ-NNNN*
Durable same-directory local-conflict copies created when
.B sync.local_conflict
is
.BR backup .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/onedrive-cpp/accounts/<display-name>--<user-id-hash>/refresh_token
Per-account OAuth refresh token. The file is created with owner-only
permissions.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/onedrive-cpp/accounts/<display-name>--<user-id-hash>/drives/<drive-name>--<drive-id-hash>/items.sqlite3
Per-account, per-Drive SQLite synchronization-state database.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/onedrive-cpp.service
systemd user service installed by the package.
.SH LOGGING
Diagnostics are written to standard error. A systemd user service captures
that stream in the journal. Follow service logs with:
.PP
.RS
.B journalctl \-\-user \-u onedrive-cpp.service \-f
.RE
.PP
OAuth tokens, device codes, authorization headers, and passwords must not be
written to logs. Take care before sharing debug output because local paths and
file metadata may still be sensitive.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to determine default configuration, state, and synchronization paths.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully, or help/version information was shown.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime, configuration, authentication, storage, or synchronization error
occurred.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid, or synchronization safely completed with one or
more blocked items.
.SH EXAMPLES
Authorize a Microsoft account:
.PP
.RS
.B onedrive-cpp auth \-\-config ~/.config/onedrive-cpp/config.toml
.RE
.PP
Inspect synchronization inputs:
.PP
.RS
.B onedrive-cpp sync \-\-dry\-run \-\-log\-level debug
.RE
.PP
Download one remote file:
.PP
.RS
.B onedrive-cpp download Documents/report.pdf
.RE
.PP
Run the monitor with a rotating log file:
.PP
.RS
.B onedrive-cpp monitor \-\-log\-file ~/.local/state/onedrive-cpp/onedrive-cpp.log
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Protect the state directory and refresh token as account credentials. Do not
publish tokens, device codes, authorization headers, private file names, or
file contents in logs or issue reports.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.PP
Project documentation:
.UR https://github.com/cloudfyy/onedrivecpp
.UE
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at:
.UR https://github.com/cloudfyy/onedrivecpp/issues
.UE
.PP
Do not include passwords, OAuth tokens, device codes, or private file contents
in a bug report.
